Dynatrace Knowledge Transfer (KT) Notes

1. Executive Summary

The KT sessions covered the operational lifecycle of Dynatrace monitoring from configuration through troubleshooting. The main themes were regression visual monitoring, synthetic HTTP monitor management, new server onboarding, firewall and OneAgent installation coordination, RUM/application detection, change tracking, and incident/problem investigation.

Regression monitoring needs periodic cleanup so broken synthetic monitors are identified and corrected before they hide application problems.

The AMD portal-* tag on HTTP monitors is a key dashboard classification mechanism: it determines which dashboard pane a monitor appears in.

New servers should be tracked centrally, approved, network-enabled, provisioned with OneAgent, validated, and only then enabled for active monitoring at the appropriate cutover point.

RUM is used differently depending on the scope of the problem: application-specific RUM is useful for a particular user/application issue, while host-level monitoring is useful for broader infrastructure/resource problems.

All Dynatrace changes should be recorded in the shared tracker so the team maintains a reliable change history across production and non-production environments.

Dynatrace Problems should be investigated using current state plus historical trends before escalating; persistent or sustained problems require action, while short-lived/transient events may not.

2. End-to-End Operating Flow

The combined KT flow can be used as the high-level operating model:

1. Identify a monitoring or server requirement.

2. Determine environment/scope (production, non-production, regression; application, site, host, or synthetic monitor).

3. For production changes, create and obtain the required change approvals/tickets before the change is performed.

4. Complete firewall/network prerequisites and obtain the appropriate Dynatrace/OneAgent installation information.

5. Configure the Dynatrace object (synthetic monitor, host monitoring mode, application detection/RUM, etc.).

6. Validate that the new or changed configuration is reporting correctly in Dynatrace.

7. Enable/disable monitoring at the correct cutover point where a server replacement is involved.

8. Update the shared tracker with the completed change and status.

9. Continue periodic dashboard/problem review and investigate sustained issues.

3. Visual Monitoring / Synthetic HTTP Monitoring

3.1 Dashboard classification using AMD portal-* tags

A central point from the KT session is that the dashboard is driven by monitor criteria/tags. For an HTTP synthetic monitor to appear in the intended dashboard pane, it needs the appropriate AMD portal-* tag.

Key note: The AMD portal-* tag is not just descriptive metadata in this dashboard design; it is used to control which pane/tile the monitor appears in.

Example discussed: AMD portal-EVV LTC places the monitor in the corresponding EVV LTC area/pane.

The team also reviewed availability-level tags such as AMDR and availability level indicators. These are separate from the dashboard placement tag and are used for other monitoring/classification purposes discussed in the session.

