3.3 Procedure: create a new HTTP synthetic monitor

1. Open the Synthetic monitoring area in the correct management zone/environment.

2. Create an HTTP monitor and provide the monitor name or URL. The KT demonstration used a test-style monitor name such as "AMD test".

3. Add at least the required baseline tag(s), including the AMDR tag discussed in the session.

4. Configure the HTTP request and target URL.

5. Apply the team standard settings discussed in the session: accept any SSL certificate, use a 60-second timeout, and configure the required successful HTTP status handling (the transcript references the team treating 401 as successful and changing the default success-code behavior accordingly).

6. Select the appropriate execution location. The discussion referenced TPC/SDC/ADC production locations depending on environment.

7. Use an approximately 2-minute execution frequency when the monitor is intended to support the dashboard view, consistent with the existing monitor pattern discussed.

8. Create/save the monitor.

9. Add the correct AMD portal-* dashboard tag so the monitor is classified into the intended pane.

10. Wait for at least one execution / several minutes and verify that the monitor appears in the expected dashboard location.

Configuration note: Exact location names, management zones, and environment mappings should be checked against the current Dynatrace configuration. The KT session demonstrated the concept and existing patterns rather than providing a complete configuration matrix.

3.4 Regression monitoring cleanup

Review regression synthetic monitors that are currently failing or no longer functioning.

For each failure, identify the actual HTTP/error response and investigate the underlying application/network cause.

Do not treat a dashboard placement problem as the same thing as an execution failure.

Capture screenshots during the regression run as requested in the session, and consolidate them into one team document.

Use regression as an early-warning environment: the goal is to restore enough coverage that important failures are visible before production.

