5. RUM and Application Detection

The second KT session demonstrated how to understand and configure application detection rules and how those rules control the scope of Real User Monitoring (RUM).

5.1 Application detection concept

A Dynatrace application can be associated with traffic by using application detection rules.

The session demonstrated a rule based on URL start/host matching.

For a specific application such as a PA/prior-authorization application, the URL/detection rule identifies the relevant application traffic inside the broader site/domain.

The discussion used examples involving secure and operations sites to explain how application scope maps to the site/domain structure.

5.2 Application-level vs site-level RUM

Scope

Use case

KT caution / note

Specific application

Turn on/off or configure RUM for a targeted application using its detection rule.

Preferred when only one application needs monitoring.

Entire site/domain

Enable RUM broadly for applications under a site.

The session specifically cautioned that high-traffic secure sites may make broad enablement undesirable.

5.3 Practical RUM configuration flow

1. Navigate to the appropriate Frontend/Web and mobile monitoring/application detection area.

2. Identify the existing application that is closest to the requirement and inspect its detection rule before creating a new one.

3. Review the application detection rule and confirm the URL/host pattern used to associate traffic with the application.

4. For a new application, add an application detection item and configure the appropriate URL/host condition.

5. Once the application is detected, use the application settings to enable or disable RUM as required.

6. Validate that the application appears and that user sessions are being collected in the intended scope.

Access action: The KT specifically identified the need to request non-production AMDR configuration access so the team can practice RUM changes safely in regression.

6. Host Monitoring Mode: Infrastructure vs. Full Stack

The KT demonstrated that the host monitoring mode can be reviewed/changed from the host settings area.

1. Open the target host.

2. Open Settings / Host monitoring.

3. Review the current monitoring mode.

4. Select the required mode, such as Infrastructure monitoring or Full Stack monitoring, based on the application/team requirement.

5. Record the change in the shared tracker.

Change-history note: The session explicitly recommended recording monitoring-mode changes in the tracker, including switches between infrastructure and Full Stack monitoring.

7. Troubleshooting and Debugging

7.1 Start with the problem statement

A single-user issue and a system-wide slowness issue should not be investigated in exactly the same way.

For an individual-user issue, start from RUM/user session information.

For application-wide slowness, start from the affected host/server and infrastructure resource indicators.

7.2 Troubleshoot a specific user/session with RUM

1. Go to the relevant Frontend/RUM application.

2. Identify the user/session associated with the reported problem.

3. Set the time range to the date/time reported by the user.

4. Open the relevant session/request.

5. Perform waterfall analysis to see the underlying requests.

6. Inspect individual requests for duration, status and errors.

7. Use View Trace where available to drill into downstream activity.

8. Use the trace to identify slow/error-producing Web API, database, AD or other calls and provide that evidence to the application team.

Debugging example from the session: The trace walkthrough showed that a user request could include Web API activity, multiple database calls, an AD call, and multiple Service Fabric calls. Service Fabric was described as comparatively black-box in this setup, meaning its internal processing may not be visible to the same depth as instrumented Web API/database activity.

7.3 Troubleshoot application-wide slowness

1. Identify the affected web/application server(s).

2. Review CPU utilization and look for sustained or abnormal peaks.

3. Review memory utilization and look for sustained pressure.

4. Review worker thread counts where available.

5. Check the internal portal performance dashboard discussed in the KT.

6. If the infrastructure indicators suggest a resource problem, contact the appropriate application/support SME and provide the evidence collected.

7.4 Worker threads and known patterns

The session described high worker thread count as a useful indicator of a possible application/resource problem.

A known system that normally exhibits high thread counts may not require repeated escalation if the behavior is already understood and documented.

Unexpected or newly elevated thread counts should be investigated in context rather than treated as an automatic fault.

8. Dynatrace Problems: When to Investigate vs. Escalate

The KT emphasized trend-based investigation before escalating every problem notification.

1. Open the problem and understand what resource/application is affected.

2. Check whether the problem is still active or was only a brief transient event.

3. Review approximately the last 7 days to see whether it is a first occurrence or a recurring pattern.

4. If needed, extend the view to approximately 30 days for additional context.

5. Consider the severity and magnitude of the issue (for example, a small error-rate increase may behave differently from a sustained high error rate).

6. If the issue is persistent, worsening, or clearly actionable, raise it with the appropriate team/SME and provide the evidence.

7. If it was brief and already resolved, document/observe as appropriate rather than automatically paging someone.

8.1 Examples discussed in the KT

A low-disk-space problem that remains for many days should be raised to the responsible portal/application team so space can be freed.

A short-lived burst of errors that disappears quickly may not require immediate escalation if no ongoing impact remains.

A recurring problem that appears repeatedly over 7-30 days should be interpreted as a pattern and investigated accordingly.

If an external/managed platform owns the component, the team should route the issue to the responsible owner rather than treating it as an internally actionable application defect.

